18:## How this is used (the design rule)
19:
20:1. **Guess at §3.0, label at §3.2.** The six why-questions (method 3.0) end with a *provisional* category — a
21:   guess from the prompt text that feeds the tiny test and a first lookup. After the algorithm is written (method
22:   3.2) its steps are grouped into pieces, one deliverable each, and **each piece is labelled** from this table
23:   (changed 2026-10-02 at EJ's request; n=0). The piece labels replace the guess; a difference is a Log line.
24:   A label says what kind of work a piece is, not that it needs its own agent: same-engine pieces with no independent
25:   check between them merge into one node (method 3.2, "a label is not a node").
26:   Checked against facts per piece: a piece that touches product code, schema, UI or config is never labelled
27:   read-only. A wrong label is a **stop and replan**, not a repair (the blueprint kind-guard's shape).
28:2. **Default-first.** For each labelled piece look up its row (or the combination's pipeline), adapt it to the
29:   specifics, then run the normal gates. Steps 2–4 of the method still run. `others` has no default: the full
30:   method from first principles.
31:3. **Challenger (the 20% rule).** A bespoke design replaces the default only with a written claim **before the
32:   run**: ≥20% lower projected run cost (tokens / agents / wall time) **at equal coverage** — same acceptance
33:   criteria ids, same checks, same blindness. Coverage is a gate, never an axis; nobody trades completeness for
34:   speed. "Ask three times, get three different answers" is variance, not signal — the default stands unless
35:   the margin is claimed and proven on paper first.
36:4. **Two-layer review of the decision.**
37:   - **Script layer (free, deterministic):** `scripts/design_gate.py` — structure, category-vs-facts, label agreement (G8: the design's
38:     categories equal the categories its nodes carry; the in-run review node is exempt), the
39:     margin arithmetic, coverage equality, pipeline well-formedness, the reviewer-kind rule, ledger rows.
40:   - **Checklist layer (cheap judgment):** a Sonnet 5.5 subagent, fresh session, blind to the designer's
41:     reasoning, judges a fixed 4-item checklist **in one pass**: does the category fit the challenge's intent?
42:     is the default pattern wrong for this instance, and why? does the claimed margin have a real basis? is
43:     anything needed absent from both designs? Verdicts binary with evidence refs — never "try to reject it".
44:     Two repair attempts on script failure; a checklist "no" escalates per the tier exit (fresh stronger node
45:     or EJ), never a third round. **Risk tier:** when the design builds or EJ flagged high risk, the checklist
46:     reviewer is a different kind from the designer (Codex `gpt-6.1-sol`) instead of Sonnet 5.5.
47:   - This reviews the *design decision*. The design's own in-run review nodes are separate and follow the
48:     `code review` row below.
49:5. **Ledger.** Every run appends one row to `docs/TASK_TYPES_LEDGER.md`: date, run id, category,
50:   default-or-challenger, claimed margin + basis, actual cost, coverage outcome, reconciled. A challenger that
51:   beats its projection twice becomes a candidate new default; a default repeatedly overridden gets its row
52:   rewritten. This is how the table earns its n.
