396:9. **Research is written to files.** The *researcher* reads docs, papers and the web (WebSearch, WebFetch). A
397:   research node writes the full findings to
398:   `docs/research/<date>-<topic>/FINDINGS.md` (each finding with an id, a source and a label) and returns only a
399:   digest of about 15 lines, no more than 20, plus the path (`docs/workflow-templates/research-file.md`). The COO
400:   reads the digest; to settle a doubt she reads one finding by id, or sends a small verify node. A design decided
401:   from the research goes to `decisions.md` (4).
402:10. **Exploring existing code is its own role** (EJ, 2026-10-03; n=0). The *explorer* reads the existing system —
403:   code, tests, configs, git history — never the web. The two roles differ in source, tools, evidence and how a
404:   claim is checked (`docs/EXECUTOR_KINDS.md`, roles). Its contract:
405:   - **Read-only**, under a stated budget (tool calls or tokens), favouring recall inside the affected area and
406:     never a repo-wide overview (TASK_TYPES, case C).
407:   - **Every claim carries file:line and a short verbatim quote**, and `scripts/quote_check.py` confirms each quote
408:     is at its line (`docs/workflow-templates/explorer-file.md`); a quote that fails is a failed finding, not a typo.
409:   - **Every behaviour claim is marked `read` or `ran`.** `read` = inferred from the code; `ran` = confirmed by
410:     running it (a test, a differential run, `~/skills/verify-extraction`). Only `ran` claims may feed a node that
411:     builds; a `read` claim a build depends on is an unclear spot until it is run.
412:   - It returns a digest and the path, like research: the impact map (case C), the old system's behaviours and
413:     golden outputs (case B), or the findings for use cases #1 and #2.
414:   - The COO explores herself when the affected area is small and fits her context (whale rule); an explorer node
415:     is for a large or unfamiliar codebase.
416:11. **Every run leaves a feedback record** (added 2026-10-02; n=0; `docs/research/20261002-workflow-feedback`).
417:   During the run, `scripts/runlog.py exec` wraps each Codex, `qwen` or `agy` call (start, end, exit, duration;
418:   raw output to the gitignored `runs/<id>/raw/`) and `runlog.py verdict` writes one line per node check. After
419:   it, `scripts/harvest_run.py` reads what the engines already recorded (Workflow run JSON and agent transcripts,
420:   Codex rollouts joined by time window and cwd, never guessed) into `runs/<id>/feedback.jsonl` and prints a
421:   digest whose last line is the ledger's Actual cost. Tokens are kept in parts (new input, cache read, output):
422:   cache reads are often ten times the new input. Only ids, numbers, paths and status are written; the repo is
423:   public. Every parser fails loudly when an engine changes its format.
424:12. **Design decisions cite their evidence** (EJ, 2026-10-03; n=0). Model memory goes stale, so every design
425:   decision cites the finding ids it rests on — research (item 9) or exploration (item 10). A decision with no
426:   source is marked *model knowledge* and treated like a `read` claim: it may not drive a node that builds until it
427:   is checked against a source or by running code. The researcher and explorer gather; the COO designs, so their
428:   picks do not become the design without a second look. Which source comes first depends on the case: the
429:   codebase for a feature in an existing system (TASK_TYPES case C), current docs for a new stack (case D).
430:
431:## 4. State and memory
432:
433:The state lives in a file (`docs/workflow-templates/state.md`). Each agent reads it, does one step, writes the new
434:state back; the next agent, or another tool, continues from there, and a broken run resumes from the last written
435:state. One writer at a time. The state file holds progress and results, never a worker's reasoning; a worker's
436:detailed notes go in its own output file, so a verifier can be kept blind to them.
437:
438:Kinds of memory when running in Claude Code or Codex with subagents:
439:
440:| Memory | Limit |
441:|---|---|
442:| Context window | Temporary; a subagent starts with only its prompt; long sessions get summarised |
443:| `CLAUDE.md` / `AGENTS.md` | Loaded every session, so keep them short and let them point to the rest |
444:| Claude Code auto-memory | Only Claude Code reads it |
445:| **Files in the repo** | The one memory every tool and every agent can read — use it for anything that must last |
446:| Skills | How to do things, not facts about a task |
447:| Run records (workflow `journal.jsonl`, the Ancient Games journal) | Nobody reads them unless asked |
448:| A subagent's final report | All that comes back; the rest is lost unless written to a file |
449:
450:Working rules: each agent gets a brief with only what its piece needs (with a template, an example and a standard, 3.8) and writes its full result to a file; research and decided designs are files too (3.8, `docs/research/`, `decisions.md`); the
451:main agent keeps conclusions, not details; stored knowledge carries a date and is rechecked before it is relied on;
452:knowledge (stable) is kept apart from state (changes every run).
453:
454:**Project memory for software work (added 2026-10-02 at EJ's request; n=0).** A release note is written for users
455:after the fact; it tells an agent what shipped, not why or what to avoid, so it is an output here, not the
456:memory. A product keeps three small append-only files beside its blueprint, each existing to answer one listed
457:query (the rule above: no store without a query):
458:
459:| File (`docs/blueprint/`) | Answers | Entry (kept to about 5 lines) | Written by, when |
460:|---|---|---|---|
461:| `decisions.md` | "Why did we choose X? What did we reject?" | date · decision · why · alternatives rejected · the R/N ids it serves · source (finding ids, or *model knowledge*, 3.8 item 12) | the COO, when a decision spot is answered (3.3) or a design choice is made; EJ's answers are the decisions |
462:| `changelog.md` | "What did run N change, and did it pass?" | date · run id · what changed · criteria covered (R1.1…) · commits · baseline result · optional `release note:` line for users | the COO, once at the end of each run that builds, from the state file |
463:| `lessons.md` | "What goes wrong around module Y?" | date · where (path or module) · the trap · how it was found · recheck by (a date or a trigger) | any node proposes, the COO appends; an entry past its recheck is marked stale, never silently trusted |
464:
465:Working rules: append only, never rewritten (a reversal is a new entry that points at the old one); every entry
466:carries a date and, where it applies, a requirement id or a path, so an agent finds it by `grep`, not by reading the
467:file; readiness (3.1, information) and the design read only the entries for the sections, ids and paths the task
468:touches; the blueprint's `README.md` map lists the files so an agent knows they exist. A release note, when one is
469:needed, is generated from the `release note:` lines of the changelog, not written separately. The qwen findings of
470:2026-10-02 (silent model substitution, exit 0 with no file) are examples of lessons.
